Write $\underline H_w=\sum_{y\le w}h_{y,w}H_y$ for the expansion of the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis element in the standard basis and $\underline H_x\underline H_y=\sum_z\mu^z_{x,y}\underline H_z$ for the structure constants of the Kazhdan-Lusztig basis. Then:

(1) $h_{y,w}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}[v]$ for all $y\le w$; equivalently the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials $P_{y,w}(q)=v^{-(\ell(w)-\ell(y))}h_{y,w}(v)$ with $q=v^{-2}$ have nonnegative integer coefficients, and in particular the $\mu$-coefficients $\mu(y,w)=[v]h_{y,w}$ are nonnegative;

(2) $\mu^z_{x,y}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}[v^{\pm1}]$: the structure constants are nonnegative, and $\mu^z_{x,y}$ is the character of the multiplicity space of $B_z^R$ in the tensor product $B_x^R\otimes_SB_y^R$, i.e. its graded multiplicity;

(3) the expansion of the product $\operatorname{ch}(B_x)\operatorname{ch}(B_y)$ in the standard basis has nonnegative coefficients, namely the $\Delta$-multiplicities of $B_xB_y$.

No positivity of Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials is assumed anywhere; the corollary is the local proof of the positivity clause from the character theorem.

Fix $x,s$ with $xs>x$ and assume $\mathrm{S}(y)$ and $\mathrm{HR}(y)$ for all $y<xs$; fix embeddings and the induced forms, a reduced expression $\underline x$ of $x$ and a generator $c_{\mathrm{bot}}$ of the one-dimensional space $\overline{B_y^R}^{-\ell(y)}$. Then:

1. the evaluation map $\mathrm{ev}:\operatorname{Hom}^0(B_y^R,M)\to\overline M^{-\ell(y)}$, $f\mapsto\overline f(c_{\mathrm{bot}})$, where $M=B_x^RB_s^R$ and $\overline f$ is the induced map on right reductions, is injective with image contained in the primitive subspace $P^{-\ell(y)}_\rho=\ker(\rho^{\ell(y)+1})$;
2. $\mathrm{ev}$ is an isometry up to the positive factor $N=\langle\rho^{\ell(y)}c_{\mathrm{bot}},c_{\mathrm{bot}}\rangle_{\overline{B_y^R}}>0$: $N(f,g)^{x,s}_y=(\mathrm{ev}(f),\mathrm{ev}(g))^{-\ell(y)}_\rho$ for all $f,g$;
3. consequently $\mathrm{HR}(x,s)$ implies $\mathrm{S}(xs)$: a definite form restricted to a subspace stays non-degenerate, so the local forms are non-degenerate and the detection theorem applies.

Assume $\mathrm S(y)$ for all $y\le x$, and let $F_x$ be a minimal Rouquier complex of a reduced expression of $x$. Then:

(1) $^0F_x\cong B_x^R$;

(2) for every $i\ge1$, $^iF_x\cong\bigoplus_{z<x}B_z^R(i)^{m_{z,i}}$ with $m_{z,i}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$, so that $F_x$ lies in $pK^b_{\le0}\cap pK^b_{\ge0}$ and is linear: every indecomposable summand of $^iF_x$ carries the internal shift exactly $i$;

(3) the multiplicities satisfy the parity vanishing $m_{z,i}=0$ unless $i\equiv\ell(x)-\ell(z)\pmod2$, and consequently, writing the standard basis element as $H_x=\sum_{z\le x}g_{z,x}\underline H_z$ (the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig coefficients), one has $g_{x,x}=1$, $g_{z,x}=\sum_i(-1)^im_{z,i}v^i$, and $(-1)^{\ell(x)-\ell(z)}g_{z,x}\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}[v^{\pm1}]$.

The statement is conditional exactly on $\mathrm S(y)$ for $y\le x$; it uses neither $\mathrm S(x')$ for $x'>x$ nor the final character theorem, and it is the induction step of the simultaneous induction.

Assume $\mathrm{S}(x)$, i.e. $\operatorname{ch}(B_x^R)=\underline H_x$. Then:

1. the space of invariant forms on $B_x^R$ is one dimensional over $\mathbb R$, and every non-zero invariant form is non-degenerate;
2. if $B_x^R$ is embedded as a direct summand of a Bott-Samelson bimodule $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)$ for a reduced expression of $x$, the restriction of the intersection form is a positive multiple of the intersection form on $B_x^R$;
3. consequently $\mathrm{HR}(x)$ holds for the intrinsic intersection form if and only if $\mathrm{HRbar}(x)$ holds for every (equivalently: some) embedding, and the two normalizations differ by a positive scalar, so no sign changes;
4. if $B=\bigoplus_zB_z^{R,m_z}$ with $\mathrm{S}(z)$ for all $z$, then non-degenerate symmetric invariant forms on $B$ are direct sums of the forms on the isotypic components tensored with nonsingular real symmetric multiplicity-space pairings, and an orthogonal decomposition into isotypic components can always be chosen.

Let $\underline x=s_1\cdots s_m$ be an expression, $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)$ its real Bott-Samelson bimodule, $\mathrm{Br}_i=\chi_i\circ\varphi_i$ the Demazure operation at the $i$-th letter, and $\operatorname{Tr},\langle-,-\rangle$ the trace and intersection form of [[def-invariant-intersection-form-on-a-bott-samelson-bimodule]]. Then:

(a) as endomorphisms of $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)$ one has $\rho\cdot(-)=\sum_{i=1}^m(s_{i-1}\cdots s_1\rho)(\alpha_{s_i}^\vee)\,\chi_i\circ\varphi_i+(-)\cdot x^{-1}\rho$ (EW Lemma 3.4), where $(-)\cdot x^{-1}\rho$ denotes right multiplication by the polynomial $x^{-1}\rho$;

(b) with the rescaled forms $\langle-,-\rangle_i':=(s_{i-1}\cdots s_1\rho)(\alpha_{s_i}^\vee)\langle-,-\rangle_i$ on $\mathrm{BS}(\widehat{\underline x}_i)$ and $\langle-,-\rangle'$ their direct sum, one has $\langle b,\rho b'\rangle_{\overline{\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)}}=\langle\varphi(b),\varphi(b')\rangle'$ for all $b,b'$ in the reduced module, where $\varphi=\sum_i\varphi_i:\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)\to\bigoplus_i\mathrm{BS}(\widehat{\underline x}_i)(1)$ (EW Lemmas 6.14 and 6.15);

(c) if $\underline x$ is reduced then every scaling factor $\gamma_i:=(s_{i-1}\cdots s_1\rho)(\alpha_{s_i}^\vee)$ is strictly positive, so the rescalings change no signs in the HR statements, and the unscattered term $(-)\cdot x^{-1}\rho$ vanishes after reduction to the Soergel module;

(d) for a reduced expression, consequently left multiplication by $\rho$ on $\overline{\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)}$ factors, up to positive rescalings, as the composite $\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)\to\bigoplus_i\mathrm{BS}(\widehat{\underline x}_i)(1)\to\mathrm{BS}(\underline x)(2)$, and this composite is the first differential of the Rouquier complex $F_{s_1}\cdots F_{s_m}$ followed by its dual map, up to the same positive rescalings.
